Navigating the Digital Frontier: A Technical Portfolio and Vision in Computer Science

Introduction: Class of 2027, Whoop!

Howdy, I am Daniel Tang from Shanghai, China. I am an aspiring computer science student, who enjoys playing piano occasionally. I am a Junior majoring in computer science at Texas A&M University, also a member of the corps of cadets class of 2027, whoop!
My journey in computer science, bridging an associate's degree from Tyler Junior College to the pursuit of a Bachelor of Science at Texas A&M University, has been a deliberate progression from theoretical understanding to the creation of impactful, real-world systems. 

Technical Portfolio: From Concept to Deployed System

My technical portfolio is built on a philosophy of solving tangible problems with robust, efficient software. Two projects exemplify this approach, spanning the domains of enterprise web systems and performance-critical systems programming.
I would like to build an Enterprise Food Delivery Management System as an end-to-end exercise in architecting for scale, specifically to replace the current Aggie Dining page. The challenge is not merely to create an ordering platform, but to design for high concurrency and real-time interaction. By leveraging Spring Boot and MySQL, I aim to establish robust core application and data layers. To address the critical 'Hot Key' problem—where frequent menu reads could overwhelm the database—I will implement a multi-layered caching strategy using Redis and Spring Cache, ensuring both high speed and data consistency.
To make the system dynamic and responsive for merchants, I will integrate WebSockets to push real-time order notifications directly to a dashboard. Furthermore, recognizing that business logic extends beyond user requests, I will utilize Spring Task to create scheduled background jobs for automated order management. This project will culminate in a professional deployment configuration using Nginx for reverse proxying and load balancing, effectively separating concerns between front-end and back-end resources. It will serve as a comprehensive lesson in full-stack engineering, data flow optimization, and the infrastructure of modern web applications.
Another project I am motivated to build is an app for students to share supportive prayers for their professors. In recent decades, political tensions have tightened on university campuses, often leading those with differing views to antagonize one another and engage in hostile discourse. I envision creating an online social platform where students can post prayers or messages of goodwill for their professors, regardless of the professor’s personal religious beliefs. I believe this gesture of respect and compassion could offer a more peaceful way to bridge divides—creating space for constructive dialogue and a kinder form of engagement.
Motivated by this vision of reducing polarization, I aim to design “Bridge,” a social platform where students can share messages of gratitude and support for professors. The primary technical challenge will be building a safe, well-moderated environment. This project will require me to expand my full-stack capabilities by learning React for a dynamic frontend, while applying and extending my Spring Boot expertise to implement secure user authentication, role-based access control, and content moderation APIs. Architecturally, the focus will be on designing for privacy—including features such as anonymous posting—and fostering positive engagement, making this a meaningful application of technology to build community.
Complementing these technical pursuits is my multicultural perspective, with native proficiency in Mandarin, conversational skills in Japanese, and professional fluency in English. This linguistic agility enhances my ability to collaborate in global technology teams and understand international markets.

